<HR size="1" color="#5d7790">
<FONT SIZE="2" FACE="Verdana, Helvetica, sans-serif">
Morning Everybody,<P>As some of you already know, I have a copy of Dark Age of Camolet, and played it for about 4 hours total yesterday.  If interested in my first opinions continue to read, otherwise hit the back button.    <IMG SRC="smile.gif" border="0"><P>Disclaimer:  I have an OLD machine (AMD K6-400, 192MB RAM, 32MB ATI Radeon Video Card) so some of what I experienced can most likely be attributed to that.  Those of you with better systems may not have a problem.<P>Installing:  Installing this was a piece of cake, as most software is these days.<P>Registering:  GAH!  WHY do I have to put in a 35 character alphanumeric CD key twice?!?! Once that is done, its a peice of cake. hehe<P>Logging in:  Login in takes about one third the time as it does for me to log into EQ.  There is even a "QuickStart" option if you have a character already created.  Quickstart lets you put in your Login name and Password, then select the char you want to play from a drop down box.  Then you log straight into the game, skipping the whole server selection and character selection parts.<P>Character Creation:  Again, pretty easy stuff, interface is nice, looks real good.  Doesn't take too long.  No choice of starting city whatsoever that i could see.<P>The Game!:  Now for the good stuff, inside the actual game.    <IMG SRC="smile.gif" border="0">  My first character was a Mage in Hibernia.  I logged into the game and was greeted by... 100+ people in my immediate vicinity, lag was terrible.  Well, not really lag, but "chop"  I would try to turn a little to the left, and BAM!  i'd turn 180 degrees in one shot.  was HELL trying to manuever in the city.  Once I figured out a few essential keys I decided to try and leave the city, and see if the video chop got better.  I didn't have to go far before I noted an extreme difference in my ability to control my character.  On the outskirts of town I opened up on a frog with my first spell, (Don't recall what it was called, but kinda lightning bolt'ish).  Worked just like i'm used to, except... no refresh time.  I could nuke and nuke and nuke and nuke until I ran out of "power", or until I got hit.    <IMG SRC="smile.gif" border="0">  The nukes seemed to do fair damage against the blue frogs I was killing.  But something just wasn't right.  I gave up on the mage after he hit level 2, not feeling overly impressed with him.<P>Insert EQ Gaming time here (Grats on the dings Eklah and Meth)<P>Back to The Game!:  With my first short experience with DAoC, I was less than impressed.  I figured, I'll log on and try a different Realm, see what the chars look like, etc.  <P>Since it seems Wardak will be a creation that follows me to my grave, I decided to create him as a Viking (closest I could really find to a Barbarian).  So Wardak is born again, this time as a Norseman Viking from Midgard.  Fear me!  hehe  Again, the video chop when i logged in, but not as bad as before, i'm thinking less people around me, ie less polys to draw.  I found my trainer, set up my "hotkeys" and headed into the fray.  Again, once outside of town the lag was pretty much a non-issue.  The newbie mobs were plentiful, as were the newbies.  Remember when you first logged into EQ, and you didn't know what ANYthing was, you didn't know that attacking that fire beetle was gonna piss off all his buddies and you'd die in a couple seconds if you hit him?  I was tentative about attacking anything when another of its kind was nearby, but after observing some people killing a wolf right next to another one, i said well, lets try it.  Unless a mob has a faction attached to it, it appears they remain neutral to you unless you are attacking it.  ie  I'm attacking Wolf A, its pissed, but Wolf B walks by and could care less.  Seemed kinda strange, but I appreciated it.    <IMG SRC="smile.gif" border="0">  I'm sure things will change as I start taking on higher level mobs.  We shall see.  I killed some lynx cubs and got to level 2, then killed some snakes, got to level 3, killed some wolves, got to level 4, then decided maybe I should sell the loot I had gathered, and see if I could afford anything.  Well, I couldn't find a single weapon that I could afford in the village I had started in, even though I had accumulated a whole 13 silver.  Off in the distance I could see a much larger town, so I ran over there to see if I could find some better weapons merchants for my level of poverty.  I found one, and bought a shiny new bronze 2 handed axe.  By this time it was getting QUITE late (130am) and figured I'd try out my new axe, then head to bed.  Well, my newbie axe was doing a whopping 5-6 points of damage, and not too fast i might add.  My new Bronze 2 Handed Axe of Slaying!! did an incredible 22-25 points of damage.  DAMN did those newbie mob parts fly. hehe  Since it was so late, and I knew if I stuck around any longer i wouldn't get ANY sleep.  I sat down and camped Wardak for the time.  <P>Summary:  I thought the graphics were incredible in comparison to EQ.  No more triangular shaped hills, no more cookie cutter PC looks, etc.  I saw several hundred different chars while playing Wardak, and the sheer number of unique looking armor sets, weaponry, spell effects, etc, blew me away.  Lag was good except in cities, but i think faster systems than mine will have less of an issue with that.  <P>At this point, given the choice between logging onto EQ or DAoC.  I'll be logging onto DAoC.  See you in Midgard!    <IMG SRC="smile.gif" border="0"><p>[ October 12, 2001: Message edited by: Lownar/Kalaan/Rakasak ]<P>--------------------<P>Wardak - 8th Level Norseman Warrior<BR>Gawaine Server<BR>Kruunk - 12th Level Firbolg Hero<BR>Galahad Server<P>Lownar, Kalaan, Rakasak - Retired
